WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.724 --> 00:00:03.504
- People ask me this, you know, for my day job,

00:00:03.504 --> 00:00:06.590
"Isn't what you do tedious?"

00:00:06.590 --> 00:00:08.423
As a stop-motion animator,

00:00:08.423 --> 00:00:10.840
you're very conscious of time

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:14.230
and of incrementally moving

00:00:15.978 --> 00:00:19.868
a three-dimensional
object bit by bit by it.

00:00:19.868 --> 00:00:22.120
“Mad God” is a stop-motion film

00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:25.975
that I've been kind of
making for like the last 30 years.

00:00:25.975 --> 00:00:29.414
There's nothing stopping me
from continuing doing it.

00:00:29.414 --> 00:00:31.081
You know, forever.

00:00:35.818 --> 00:00:38.529
When I was five years old in 1955,

00:00:38.529 --> 00:00:41.366
I saw King Kong on television.

00:00:41.366 --> 00:00:43.319
As a stupid kid, I was just enthralled

00:00:43.319 --> 00:00:46.918
by Hollywood monsters, and
that was really the thing

00:00:46.918 --> 00:00:49.712
that kind of kindled my appetite.

00:00:49.712 --> 00:00:52.877
Subsequently, over the
years, I have worn many hats

00:00:52.877 --> 00:00:54.940
in the motion picture racket.

00:00:54.940 --> 00:00:59.918
I've been involved with Star
Wars, the “RoboCop” series,

00:00:59.918 --> 00:01:03.068
Jurassic Park, Starship
Troopers, on and on.

00:01:03.068 --> 00:01:04.355
One of the things that gives me

00:01:04.355 --> 00:01:07.605
a lot of pleasure is, is to make something,

00:01:08.552 --> 00:01:12.239
but the day job has
certain requirements to it.

00:01:12.239 --> 00:01:14.765
Over the years, those
requirements have become

00:01:14.765 --> 00:01:16.432
a lot more limiting.

00:01:19.479 --> 00:01:22.733
The “Mad God” project
allowed me to just make

00:01:22.733 --> 00:01:26.582
this more pure thing that
was kind of a form of therapy

00:01:26.582 --> 00:01:30.208
for me as an antidote to my day job.

00:01:30.208 --> 00:01:32.014
It's a combination of live-action

00:01:32.014 --> 00:01:36.554
and stop-motion miniature
sets in layers like a collage.

00:01:36.554 --> 00:01:39.784
For lack of a better term,
it's a passion project.

00:01:39.784 --> 00:01:43.951
And things keep getting
deeper and darker and odder.

00:01:46.325 --> 00:01:49.207
There are imaginary cities that are built

00:01:49.207 --> 00:01:51.306
out of iron ingots.

00:01:51.306 --> 00:01:54.786
There's strange jungles
that you go through.

00:01:54.786 --> 00:01:58.953
There's this huge dystopic,
postindustrial nightmare city.

00:02:00.919 --> 00:02:03.094
And it just goes on and on and on,

00:02:03.094 --> 00:02:05.010
practically anything that I can think of.

00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:08.351
Inherit in it, there's a, um, level of surprise

00:02:08.351 --> 00:02:12.560
that I think, you know, for me, is a
very child-like kind of effect.

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:14.880
I shoot here at my studio in Berkeley.

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:18.130
It's stocked with all kinds
of stuff for all kinds

00:02:18.130 --> 00:02:20.836
of different electronic
projects, woodworking projects,

00:02:20.836 --> 00:02:24.501
lights, cameras, so it's a huge toy box.

00:02:24.501 --> 00:02:27.719
All of the characters in
“Mad God” are built by me.

00:02:27.719 --> 00:02:30.549
The process begins with
the articulated skeleton,

00:02:30.549 --> 00:02:32.240
and that's made by ball-and-socket

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:35.213
or hinge joints that allow you to move

00:02:35.213 --> 00:02:38.128
the characters incrementally
one frame at a time.

00:02:38.128 --> 00:02:41.324
And then I just wrap
lengths of polyurethane foam

00:02:41.324 --> 00:02:44.640
and build up the general mass.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:47.701
From there, I will take latex skins,

00:02:47.701 --> 00:02:51.096
adhere them with rubber
cement, paint the thing,

00:02:51.096 --> 00:02:52.697
and then it's ready to shoot.

00:02:52.697 --> 00:02:55.962
When you're actually
shooting a stop-motion scene,

00:02:55.962 --> 00:02:59.149
you project into the character.

00:02:59.149 --> 00:03:01.706
And sometimes it can get very complicated.

00:03:01.706 --> 00:03:05.797
You are always gauging
acceleration and deceleration,

00:03:05.797 --> 00:03:10.370
weight and mass, and then
that kind of leads to:

00:03:10.370 --> 00:03:14.120
But you know what, what
would make it better?

00:03:15.272 --> 00:03:18.688
For me, this whole process
is more of a compulsion.

00:03:18.688 --> 00:03:19.953
I really have no say over it.

00:03:19.953 --> 00:03:23.120
It's just something that I, I have to do.

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:25.826
You know, I’ve been doing it for so long,

00:03:25.826 --> 00:03:28.506
I breathe, I live and
breathe in this world.

00:03:28.506 --> 00:03:30.423
It's all I think about.

00:03:33.904 --> 00:03:38.071
(loud clunking)
(hissing)

